Found a great, self-contained example — same file, same struct literal, formatted two different ways purely because of a trailing comma.

**`core/unit/mctest_mpi_comm.c`** — the original source has this exact literal repeated many times:
```c
gkyl_mpi_comm_new( &(struct gkyl_mpi_comm_inp) {
    .mpi_comm = MPI_COMM_WORLD,
    .decomp = decomp
  }
);
```

Lines 57–60 (no trailing comma after `.decomp = decomp`) vs lines 145–148 (identical, but with a trailing comma `.decomp = decomp,`). After clang-format ran (with the trailing-comma-strip step skipped), they came out completely differently:

- **No trailing comma** (original lines 57–60) → clang-format collapses it onto one line since it fits within the column limit:
  ```c
  struct gkyl_comm *comm =
    gkyl_mpi_comm_new(&(struct gkyl_mpi_comm_inp){ .mpi_comm = MPI_COMM_WORLD, .decomp = decomp });
  ```
- **With trailing comma** (original lines 145–148) → clang-format keeps every field on its own line:
  ```c
  struct gkyl_comm *comm = gkyl_mpi_comm_new(&(struct gkyl_mpi_comm_inp){
    .mpi_comm = MPI_COMM_WORLD,
    .decomp = decomp,
  });
  ```

That's exactly the behavior `ci/strip-trailing-commas.py` exists to normalize away before clang-format ever sees the file — clang-format treats a trailing comma in a brace-init list as "the author wants one element per line" (the same convention it uses for trailing commas in C++ argument lists/containers), so identical code formats inconsistently purely based on whether a stray trailing comma happens to be present. You can see the same phenomenon later in the file at lines 606–608 (comma present, one-per-line, plus fields even reordered differently) versus lines 774–775, where three fields all packed onto one line because there was no trailing comma in the source.

